          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I keep getting urine infections.",
          "answer": "Urine infections are bacterial infections of the urinary system. Most involve the bladder and urethra, which is called cystitis. *Escherichia coli* causes most of them. A kidney infection is a different and more serious illness, not a worse version of the same one (General clinical reference: Urinary tract infection).\n\nGuidance separates **uncomplicated** infection, in a non-pregnant adult with a normal urinary tract and normal immune function, from **complicated** infection. Complicated cases include pregnancy, men, catheters, structural or functional urinary problems, kidney stones, poorly controlled diabetes, and weakened immunity. These are treated differently (Uncomplicated urinary tract infection guideline). The guideline says it covers recurrence, but my search returned only its scope section. **I could not find the advice it gives on preventing repeat infections, so I can't summarise it.** The lifestyle search also returned nothing about urinary infections; its results covered diabetes, asthma, heart failure and reflux. I have no sourced prevention or lifestyle advice to offer you.\n\n**Seek urgent care now** if you have a fever, shaking, confusion, or you feel unusually unwell, especially alongside urinary symptoms. The sepsis guidance says feeling unusually unwell is often the earliest sign of a dangerous response to infection. Lower the threshold further if you are over 65, pregnant, have diabetes, have kidney or liver disease, have a catheter, or take medicines that weaken the immune system (Sepsis guidance). I could not complete a separate search on urinary red flags, so this list comes from the sepsis guidance.\n\n**See a clinician** if you keep getting these infections. A clinician can check whether any of the complicating factors above apply to you and can talk you through prevention options that suit your situation. Bring a note of how often they happen and what treatment you have had.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 11017.959626000447,
          "red_flag_matched": false,
          "red_flag_matched_raw": false,
          "red_flag_negation_suppressed": false,
          "escalation_present_pre_repair": true,
