          "question": "Is there anything that reduces how often they happen?",
          "answer": "The condition is urine infections (urinary tract infections), which is clear from your earlier message.\n\nThe guidance describes repeat infection as two or more in six months, or three or more in a year. For prevention, the uncomplicated UTI guideline lists these measures (Uncomplicated urinary tract infection guideline):\n\n- **Behavioural changes.** The guideline names them but my search did not return the specifics, so I can't list them.\n- **Vaginal oestrogen after menopause.**\n- **Antibiotic prevention**, either continuous or after sex, used only where infections are frequent and disabling. The guideline says this has to be weighed against antibiotic resistance. Any decision about it belongs with a clinician.\n- **Cranberry products and more fluids.** The guideline says the evidence for these is limited and mixed.\n\nI could not find any diet or lifestyle advice specific to urine infections. The diet search returned guidance on other conditions, such as blood pressure and diabetes, so I have not used it here. My final search was blocked by a tool limit, so I could not check further.\n\nPlease don't start or stop any treatment, including antibiotics or cranberry products, based on this. A clinician can check whether any of the complicating factors I mentioned earlier apply to you and can advise on which prevention measures suit you.\n\nIf you develop a fever, shaking chills, confusion, or feel unusually unwell, seek urgent care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment, condition-urinary-tract-infection, lifestyle-hypertension-diet, lifestyle-type-2-diabetes-diet]",
