          "turn_index": 2,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do I need an antibiotic every time?",
          "answer": "**Short answer: no. Guidance does not support an antibiotic for every infection.** Many common infections are viral, and antibiotics do not treat them.\n\n**When antibiotics are not needed.** For most colds, most sore throats, acute bronchitis, and early uncomplicated sinusitis, guidance says the infection is viral in most cases. Antibiotics do not shorten these illnesses or prevent complications at a rate that justifies routine use. They do cause harm: side effects, allergic reactions, disruption of normal gut bacteria, *Clostridioides difficile* infection, and resistant bacteria (Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship guidance; Upper respiratory infection reference). Antibiotics also have no activity against influenza (Influenza guidance).\n\n**Why they do not treat viral infections.** The sources state that antibiotics have no effect on viral infection. They do not explain the biology in the passages I retrieved, so I can't go further than that.\n\n**When they may be needed.** The sources describe antibiotics as the treatment for bacterial infections, such as many urinary tract infections. Even there, guidance is not uniform. Some European guidance treats mild bladder infections as a shared decision, with symptom treatment and delayed or no antibiotics, because some episodes resolve without them. Other guidance keeps immediate antibiotics as the default, citing symptom duration and the risk of kidney involvement (Uncomplicated urinary tract infection guideline). The sources treat this as a disagreement about how much antibiotic exposure to avoid, not about the facts.\n\n**Questions you might ask a clinician before taking one:**\n- Is this infection likely bacterial or viral, and how sure can we be?\n- Is a delayed prescription or a wait-and-see approach an option?\n- What symptoms would mean I should be seen again?\n- What are the side effects, and what are the risks of resistance for me?\n\nThe sources I retrieved do not contain a list of questions to ask, so this list is my suggestion, not sourced guidance.\n\n**Seek urgent care now** if you have a high fever, shaking chills, confusion, or you feel unusually unwell (Sepsis guidance).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions. Do not start or stop any antibiotic based on this.\n[sources: guideline-uri-antibiotic-stewardship, condition-upper-respiratory-infection, guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment, condition-urinary-tract-infection]",
